WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.799
Settlement options.

2
00:00:01.974 --> 00:00:04.539
When a life insurance policy pays a death claim,

3
00:00:04.668 --> 00:00:09.149
the settlement option decides how that money actually reaches the beneficiary.

4
00:00:10.092 --> 00:00:13.377
The policyowner can choose a settlement option in advance,

5
00:00:13.738 --> 00:00:16.987
or leave the choice to the beneficiary at claim time.

6
00:00:17.534 --> 00:00:22.874
The default is a lump sum: the entire death benefit, paid all at once.

7
00:00:23.421 --> 00:00:27.797
And a lump-sum death benefit is generally received free of income tax.

8
00:00:28.722 --> 00:00:30.579
With the interest only option,

9
00:00:30.603 --> 00:00:35.710
the insurer keeps the principal and pays the beneficiary just the interest it earns.

10
00:00:36.071 --> 00:00:40.598
Here's the exam point: the death benefit itself is tax-free,

11
00:00:40.959 --> 00:00:42.989
but that interest is taxable.

12
00:00:43.791 --> 00:00:46.751
Later, the principal goes to a secondary beneficiary.

13
00:00:47.692 --> 00:00:50.083
Next, the pair the exam loves to mix up.

14
00:00:50.444 --> 00:00:54.661
Under the fixed period option, you choose how long the payments last,

15
00:00:54.681 --> 00:00:59.301
say ten years, and the insurer calculates how much each payment will be.

16
00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:03.113
Under the fixed amount option, you choose the size of each payment,

17
00:01:03.133 --> 00:01:04.851
say two thousand dollars a month,

18
00:01:04.979 --> 00:01:08.055
and payments continue until the principal and interest run out.

19
00:01:08.509 --> 00:01:12.409
So whichever one you lock in, the other is calculated.

20
00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.053
Life income options pay for the rest of the beneficiary's life.

21
00:01:17.228 --> 00:01:21.093
Life only pays the largest amount, but payments stop at death.

22
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:25.760
Life with period certain guarantees payments for a minimum number of years,

23
00:01:25.935 --> 00:01:27.734
even if the payee dies early.

24
00:01:28.536 --> 00:01:33.179
Life with refund returns any unpaid principal to a secondary beneficiary.

25
00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.753
And joint and survivor keeps paying until the second of two people dies,

26
00:01:37.986 --> 00:01:40.389
so it pays the smallest amount.

27
00:01:41.332 --> 00:01:45.395
So remember the trade-off: the more the insurer guarantees,

28
00:01:45.454 --> 00:01:46.962
the smaller each payment.

29
00:01:47.416 --> 00:01:51.896
And for fixed period versus fixed amount, whatever you lock in,

30
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:53.463
the other is calculated.
